# ── Signing secrets — NEVER commit ────────────────────────────────────
# Upload keystore + creds for Play Store release signing.
# Losing the keystore = losing the ability to publish updates ever again.
# Always back up upload-keystore.jks separately on the day you create it.
android/key.properties
android/app/upload-keystore.jks
*.jks
*.keystore

# ── Service accounts / tokens ─────────────────────────────────────────
# These are real secrets: they authenticate AS the project and can read and
# write any user's data. They must never be committed.
*.env
.env.*
service-account*.json
firebase-adminsdk*.json
GoogleService-Info.plist

# google-services.json is deliberately NOT ignored.
#
# It is client configuration, not a credential: every value in it ships inside
# the APK on the Play Store, so anyone can already read them. What actually
# protects the project is the SHA-1 certificate check and the Firebase security
# rules, neither of which this file can bypass.
#
# It is tracked because it carries the SHA-1 fingerprints that make Google
# Sign-In work. When it was ignored, that configuration existed on exactly one
# machine, and a fresh clone silently built an app whose sign-in was broken —
# with nothing in the history to explain why.
#
# Note this repository is public, so the API key in it will be picked up by
# secret scanners. That is expected rather than a leak; restrict the key by
# package name and SHA-1 in Google Cloud Console so the alert stays accurate.
firebase-service-account*.json
ios/api_key.json

# ── Generated Play Store assets (regen via tools/play_assets.py) ──────
play-assets/
play-assets.zip

# ── Claude Code session artefacts ─────────────────────────────────────
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
.claude/worktrees/
